---
import TileGroup from "../TileGroup.astro";
---
<TileGroup id="projPortfolio" layout="alternative" class="mt-25 mb-40">
    <span slot="title" class="text-4xl max-[1050px]:text-2xl font-semibold font-titles"><a href="https://github.com/BHalacsy/Portfolio-Fullstack" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Fullstack portfolio</a></span>

    <img slot="media" src="media/icons/skills/nodejs.svg" alt="NodeJS" class="w-[35%] h-auto">

    <Fragment slot="secondary">
        <span class="text-lg font-medium block mb-1">Tech used</span>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            A lot of question arouse when picking the idle stack for my portfolio and since its initial build I have reconsidered it.
            Initially using vanilla <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">typescript</a>, I opted to use two frameworks for the rebuild.
            <a href="https://astro.build/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Astro</a> for statically displayed content and for more dynamic content the obvious choice of <a href="https://react.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">React</a>.
            Along with TS (Astro & React) for the frontend I opted to use <a href="https://tailwindcss.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">tailwind</a> for styling,
            as I did not mind the added clutter it adds to my html file if it meant a simplified way of styling. Along with some useful plugins that I will talk about in the description.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            For the backend I chose <a href="https://go.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Golang</a> as I had fallen in love with the language during my first internship.
            Go allowed me to use <a href="https://gin-gonic.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Gin</a> and rely on a minimal API style for my minimal application.<br>
            The database for the website is my Redis clone containerized alongside the backend of the website using <a href="https://docs.docker.com/compose/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">docker compose</a>, both of which are running on a NAS.
        </p>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="description">
        <span class="text-2xl font-semibold">Description</span>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            The website you are currently reading this on is actually the project I am going to talk about next.
            I figured after I had finished my <a href="#projRedis" class="link-text">Redis clone</a> that I should use for a real world application to show its functionality in practice.
            Having your very own website always was appealing, so when the opportunity came around to utilize my previous project in a way that can be viewed by anyone, it was really a no-brainer.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Now having a database or even a backend is not necessary for a web portfolio, but as someone who inspires to be a future software engineer
            I figured I should get a taste of full-stack web development sooner rather than later.
            Three features came to mind that could utilize my db in one way or another, a page view counter and an anonymous chatroom alongside the collaborative canvas you can find in the <a href="#toybox" class="link-text">toybox</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Once my stack and features were chosen I began really understanding how API endpoints work and focused solely at first on the backend with a very minimal frontend structure.
            As someone who aspires to become a backend developer, I was eager to begin with that so my development started from back to front.
            ASP.NET's minimal api made it a breeze to set up the various endpoints needed to communicate with my db, once that communication was established it was time for some frontend development.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            I initially considered using a framework like <a href="https://react.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">React</a> but opted not to as I figured a project with this "relatively" small scale was not necessary,
            I would later come to regret this decision as it would have familiarized myself with a widely used web framework.
            No matter, using vanilla TS was sufficient and forced me to utilize more traditional frontend techniques which is always a good skill to have.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            The very first feature and easiest was the view counter as it only increments a single number in our db on an API call.
            For the rest of the features I could not say the same, the collaborative canvas especially was a challenge and forced me to reconsider the design I had in mind.
            Due to the large amount of data we want to save and load at any given time, I opted to "chunk" the canvas into tiles for saving and into sub-strokes for real-time visualizing for other clients.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            As for frontend design choices I always consider a relatively clean and smooth look.
            Tailwind simplified the styling process to a great extent and is definitely a tool I will utilize in the future provided I work with HTML again.
            I knew I wanted a single page design with various sections, these sections initially were all one single tile,
            but I had made the decision to tile each div in the sections to get this almost mosaic layout.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Frontend plugs also came in handy as I never had experience animating elements on HTML,
            decided to use <a href="https://rombo.co/tailwind/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Rombo</a> which is a sleek and easy to use plugin just for that.
            Having these springy, blurry and sleek animations was nice, but always ran when the page had loaded which defeated their purpose, as the user would never see them.
            I always knew about the Intersect Observer API but setting it up seemed like a challenge,
            therefore I utilized an existing <a href="https://github.com/heidkaemper/tailwindcss-intersect" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">tailwind-intersect</a> plugin
            which did the very same task but much easier to implement.
            Along with some subtle animations on hover and a simple overhead menu with dark mode and casual music options, I reckon this website turned out alright.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Deployment also came with its own challenges. I knew I could run the websites backend using a NAS that our family has and on it, I ran the backend, the Redis clone and a cloudflare tunnel, so
            I wouldn't need to forward any ports and keep a high level of security for our local network. My main subdomain is used to connect the user to this page, while an api.halacsy.com domain is made to route to the NAS for api calls.
            All of these built together and managed on a docker compose file for quick build up and tear down of the website.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            For more information check out my GitHub or click the title of this section.
        </p>
    </Fragment>
</TileGroup>
